export const REMAINING = {
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Forbidden bones combine. Shach ruled like Ran that soft bones combine with the prohibition; and see responsa Maharai Halevi siman 28 who did not rule thus but rather that they combine with permitted food — see there his proof. And see there further in siman 27 where he wrote there is doubt whether specifically in cooking do forbidden bones combine with permitted food and not in salting or roasting; and therefore in a weak prohibition by law it does not require sixty except due to a stringency that we compare to cooking — they combine in any case; but a fat prohibition that requires sixty because the prohibition spreads — perhaps it does not spread in bones and they do not combine even those of permitted food — see there. And see in Sefer Teivat Gema parashat Vayera letter 9 who wrote that even a limb from a live animal that fell into permitted food — the bones in it combine with permitted food, even though in a limb from a live animal a bone combines for an olive-volume — this is a novelty; and specifically while in its eye its emission does not help — see there.`,
    '2#_': `In the gravy. See in responsa Beit Yaakov end of siman 57 who wrote that what rises as steam upward is lacking from the food, and when estimating with sixty one must estimate what is lacking through the steam — see there.`,
    '3#_': `One does not nullify a prohibition. Shach wrote in the name of poskim that it is d'rabbanan; and see in responsa Noda B'Yehuda second edition part 14 siman 45 who wrote that specifically to nullify the taste of prohibition is d'rabbanan, but the substance of prohibition dry in dry in the majority is d'oraisa, and likewise to nullify in sixty — see there (and through this is resolved what he challenged in Sefer Tiferet Yisrael on mishnayot Ma'aser Sheni chapter 2 mishnah 4 — see there; and see in Netziv what I wrote on this); but wet in wet to nullify in sixty — it appears there permitted d'oraisa; and see on this in responsa Pnei Aryeh siman 62 at length; and see in Noda B'Yehuda first edition part 14 siman 26 s.v. ve'akhpakh omer ani; and see in responsa Shemen Rokeach part 2 siman 14 who extended greatly on this matter and explained many details in this law.`,
    '4#_': `L'chatchila. [Shach in the name of Taz: that which is permitted if his intent is not specifically otherwise — in another matter. And in Pri Megadim it is explained that even if possible only with great effort it is also permitted — see there; and see in Sefer Beit Yehuda who wrote accordingly it appears regarding Pri Etz called malinsh that they cook in water and afterward squeeze the malinsh and throw them away and strain the sack and cook it in honey to dip bread and meat in them — one need not check beforehand each malinsh one by one to see inside the holes to check for worms that are common in them, since there is great effort to check all of them; but Pri Megadim's words are not compelling — see there.] And see in responsa Noda B'Yehuda part 14 siman 26 on the matter of kruvok, and some call it hozin b'la'azin — it is the bladder of an impure fish, and they dry it and put it in a liquid of honey water and its nature is to whiten the liquid; and the great ones of the generation were aroused on this that it should be forbidden, for this remains inside the liquid and is pickled like cooked; and if because it is nullified in sixty — one does not nullify prohibition l'chatchila — and he extended on this and concludes it is fully permitted for many reasons — see there; and see in responsa Tiferet Tzvi part 14 siman 73.`,
    '5#_': `For his sake. Shach in the name of Taz: he says permitted — he is b'shogeg; and see in responsa Tzemach Tzedek siman 46 who wrote that if he is in doubt in ruling and asked students and did not ask renowned decisors — he is not called b'shogeg — see there; and through this he rejected the words of responsa Beit Yaakov siman 85 who wished to compare if she acted on a woman's statement to acting on a sage's ruling — what is the difference — see there; and see further in Beit Yaakov there who disagrees with Taz on this. [And see Magen Avraham siman 318 s.k. 3; and see in responsa Chavat Sela siman 88 what he wrote on this, and the implication of his words that he agrees with Taz on this — see there.]`,
    '6#_': `But if it became known. Shach disagrees on this that one should not distinguish at all between known and not known, but between b'shogeg and b'mezid; and see further in Shach who extended to refute Issur VeHeter's view who wrote that even where we say chein nafsho there is also a distinction between known and not known that does not exist; and he raised that even without knowledge we say chein nafsho; and he concluded even Rama in Darkei Moshe and Turei Chayim brought Issur VeHeter's words that without knowledge we do not say chein nafsho — lest the Rav retracted and therefore did not write this law in his hagahot — see there; and so raised Pri Chadash and Minchat Yitzchak general rule 5 s.k. 38 — except Minchat Yitzchak wrote there that in wet in wet, since in Hagahat Pri Megadim we do not say chein nafsho in it, one may be lenient in this without knowledge — see there. And Pri Megadim wrote: and for practical halachah without knowledge in basar b'chalav certainly forbidden d'oraisa, and in other prohibitions in Hagahat Pri Megadim I have no authority to be lenient — see there; and it appears also to be stringent is not clear to him; and see in responsa Rabbeinu Akiva Eiger new edition end of seif s.k. 7 who wondered on Shach in what he wrote "perhaps the Rav retracted" — for it appears there is no doubt, for certainly the Rav retracted, for above siman 73 seif 6 in found liver clinging he wrote in the hagahah chein nafsho and needs sixty against all the fowl; and the language "found" implies they did not sense the liver remained in the fowl until afterward when found so — behold even without knowledge we say chein nafsho; and so we rule siman 72 seif 3 regarding heart. And since so, even Pri Megadim wrote it is not clear to him to be lenient in Hagahat Pri Megadim — in my humble opinion it is clear to be stringent, for in that case of heart and liver no view to be lenient in Hagahat Pri Megadim was mentioned there. And again he wrote to resolve Shach's words: since Shach wrote plainly that that piece is certainly forbidden even without knowledge — lest much prohibition remained in it — since so automatically the others are forbidden if there is not sixty against the entire piece; for though we say without knowledge we do not say chein nafsho — nevertheless after the mixture became known and the piece stands in its prohibition, the chein nafsho forbids now the other — as if it fell into another pot it would forbid, so too it forbids the pieces in this pot. And if so one may say the reason in heart and liver requires sixty against all the fowl — it deals when found clinging while the pot was still boiling; and since the fowl remained in its prohibition, now after knowledge the fowl became nevelah and forbids the others as if it fell now into another pot; but where this does not apply, such as they removed the fowl from the pot before it was known, or the pot cooled before it was known — in this Shach was in doubt whether Rama retracted or not; and on this Pri Megadim wrote it is not clear to him to be lenient in Hagahat Pri Megadim, also to be stringent not clear to him; and therefore one who is lenient in this in Hagahat Pri Megadim or for Shabbat need we do not rebuke him — so too see there.`
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#ו': `For such is the essential ruling. Bach challenged: this is a clinging prohibition; Shach answered we do not say chein nafsho since hag'alah helps. See what Peleti wrote.`,
    '5#ב': `Forbidden to sell it. Shach's view: if he does not sell at high price to a Jew, it is permitted. See Peleti.`,
    '5#ד': `Before it became known. Issur VeHeter's view: even chein nafsho we do not say before it became known in the mixture; Shach challenged and wrote that therefore Rama omitted it. See what I wrote in Peleti — also Issur VeHeter's view is in other prohibitions and not in basar b'chalav; and I settled Shach's difficulties.`,
    '5#ה': `But if it became known — see what I wrote in Peleti in resolving Shach's difficulty on Issur VeHeter.`,
    '6#ב': `One increases upon it and nullifies it. Orach Chaim regarding permitted oil ruled one may not nullify it. See Peleti — there is a distinction between wet and dry, and also between muktzeh prohibition which is like d'oraisa and other prohibitions.`,
    '6#ג': `It returns and is stirred. Pri Chadash extended at length on this root. See what Peleti wrote in all views of the poskim; for halachah one may not be lenient against Rama's words — see there.`,
    '6#ד': `For behold it was nullified in water — even according to Ran in Nedarim that heter b'heter is not nullified — nevertheless it does not become nevelah; if so, when it falls from the milk, only according to its value falls, and there is sixty — permitted.`
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Tur in the name of Rabbeinu Shimshon, and similarly Rosh agreed, and so too Rashba from Yerushalmi chapter 1 of Orlah:`,
    '1#ב': `There; and so Rashba wrote, and so Ran — that is, the reason forbidden bones in the foreleg do not combine to permit hullin — daf 98.`,
    '3#_': `Rashba in responsum, question, siman 464, and like R' Efrayim:`,
    '4#א': `The statement of R' Chanina there daf 97, and the first version is stringent, as Rashi explained, and so Rosh there and Semag:`,
    '4#ב': `Rashba in responsum and wrote: because min she'eino mino in its kind we establish that an olive-volume in keAchilat Peras is d'oraisa; and it is possible even up to sixty according to those who hold ta'am k'ikkar is d'oraisa, like R' Chayyim and those who follow his approach:`,
    '4#ג': `There, and like Rambam chapter 15 of Forbidden Foods:`,
    '5#ג': `Tur in the name of Rambam chapter 15 of Forbidden Foods, from the law of one who cooks on Shabbat, as I noted in Tur Orach Chaim siman 318 (and Rashba in the name of Rambam and Ra'avad):`,
    '6#א': `Tur in the name of Rashba (and in Beit Yosef it is brought):`,
    '7#_': `Rivash siman 249 in the name of Ra'avad; and similar to these matters Tur wrote in siman 122 in the name of Rashba, and Mechaber wrote them there too:`
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `Bones. Taz wrote: since they are not edible they are not in the category of prohibition; nevertheless they absorb through cooking — therefore they combine to nullify the prohibition. Maharach challenged Rama from what Turei Chayim wrote, that even pottery in a vessel needs sixty against it — if so, here too one should say the bones are nevelah. I say: Moses is true and his Torah is true — a bone is truly like a metal vessel, for we do not say chein nafsho in it because it has a remedy through hag'alah; likewise a bone, as explained in Orach Chaim siman 451, that bone vessels are permitted through hag'alah — therefore chein nafsho does not apply here — end of his words. Ran in chapter 98 wrote that soft bones combine with the prohibition because of marrow in them and because of their softness moisture emerges; but dry bones or eggshells and the like — even those of prohibition combine to nullify the prohibition; and so is the view of Issur VeHeter and Bach.`,
    '1#ג': `They are stringent. Nevertheless, for the prohibition too they do not combine; but the bones of permitted food combine with permitted food. Shach.`,
    '3#_': `The absorbed piece. Explanation: a piece that absorbed from prohibition that fell into permitted food, and there is not sixty in the permitted food to nullify the prohibition except through combining the piece — they also combine the piece to nullify; however the piece itself is forbidden, as Mechaber wrote at the beginning of siman 106; but we are not accustomed to practice thus — rather one needs sixty against the entire piece; and even so the piece itself is forbidden.`,
    '4#א': `And it diminished. Maharshal wrote: it appears even where we saw the prohibition fell and did not diminish by one measure together with the permitted food — nevertheless one may not estimate what the pot absorbed, for one cannot stand on the root of the matter and does not know how much the pot absorbed and how many times spoilage will come from this; rather one always estimates as it comes before us. Taz wrote: according to this one may not rule permissibly regarding what Shulchan Aruch wrote in this seif — one estimates with a good estimate, etc.`,
    '5#א': `L'chatchila. Shach wrote: some say it is forbidden to nullify it d'oraisa; however b'dieved all agree its law is as Mechaber wrote afterward — end of his words. Taz wrote: in siman 84 seif 13 it is explained that if his intent is only for another matter, such as to fix honey, it is permitted; and in siman 137 seif 2 I wrote this is specifically when it cannot be done otherwise.`,
    '5#ב': `He transgressed. Explanation: he threw a small amount of prohibition into abundant permitted food where there is sixty against it, or it fell by itself when there was not sixty in the permitted food but he increased afterward.`,
    '5#ג': `For his sake. For if one permits for one for whom it was nullified for his sake, we are concerned lest he come to say to a non-Jew or a servant to nullify it — therefore we penalize him; but if it was not his and he also did not intend to nullify for himself, it is permitted for him, for his bad act does not help for a Jew. Rashal wrote: specifically when this one knew it was nullified for him it is pleasing to him even if he did not command him to nullify it; but if he did not know from him, it is permitted for him, for he is like one who acted b'shogeg; and where it is forbidden for him, it is forbidden also for his household members. Taz wrote: if he erred in law and thought it is permitted to nullify the prohibition, he is called b'shogeg; proof from Shabbat chapter Kol HaGadol in the mishnah — if he forgot there is a prohibition of melachah in this, even though he knows today is Shabbat, he is called b'shogeg — likewise here; and so is stated explicitly in Tosafot Bekhorot daf 23 s.v. svar, etc.`,
    '5#ה': `Nevelah. Shach wrote: at first glance this is difficult — what connection does this have to chein nafsho? For even according to those who do not say chein nafsho in other prohibitions, nevertheless a piece that absorbed prohibition and permitted food was added afterward — the piece itself remains in its prohibition, as all poskim wrote at the beginning of siman 110, that the prohibition in it is not entirely emitted; rather the Rav comes to teach us that a piece that absorbed prohibition and permitted food was added afterward — even the addition is forbidden because there is not sixty against the entire piece; but if one increased b'shogeg sixty against all that is nevelah, even in basar b'chalav wet in wet all is permitted; but in a piece that absorbed prohibition, even if one increased sixty against the entire piece, the piece itself remains in its prohibition. He also wrote: from the Rav's words it appears that even in basar b'chalav we do not say chein nafsho wet in wet, and he disagrees with him — see there; he left Rama's words requiring study.`,
    '5#ו': `They practiced. Taz wrote: the reason is we need not be concerned lest more was added after it became nevelah — we do not establish prohibition presumptively; however if the teacher knows more was added but it is not known when — in such a case certainly one must investigate when it was added, as I wrote for halachah — end of his words. Shach wrote: even according to those who hold wet in wet is nevelah, and likewise a piece that absorbed prohibition — for all of them we are not accustomed to investigate, for we do not establish prohibition presumptively; however what the Rav wrote to distinguish between whether the mixture is known or not — and even in chein nafsho there is this distinction — Shach disagrees, for according to those who hold chein nafsho, immediately when there was not sixty it gave taste — what difference if it is known or not; and so is proven from siman 92 regarding inserting a dairy spoon into a meat pot twice without knowing in between — one needs sixty both times, because the first time the entire spoon became nevelah; and so several other proofs — see there.`,
    '6#א': `One increases. Shach challenged: in Orach Chaim siman 477 Mechaber wrote that the oil in a Chanukah lamp that became mixed with other oil and there is not sixty to nullify it — some say one may not add to it to nullify it — end of his words; behold Chanukah lamp oil is only rabbinic — he forced to distinguish that there, since it was designated for its mitzvah, it is stricter — requires study — end of his words. (Magen Avraham wants to resolve there that Chanukah is a matter that has a permitted use for the coming year, and I cannot understand his words — for if so, even in a thousand it would not be nullified; why did Mechaber write there "and there is not sixty to nullify it")`,
    '6#ב': `To change. Taz wrote: one may say regarding wood that fell from the palm on Yom Tov into the oven — one increases upon them prepared wood and nullifies them, for there it is lightening the prohibition and one does not benefit from them until the time of their destruction from the world, and then there is no substance in the prohibition. Maharshal wrote: that which we say there the reason is lightening — specifically because it is davar sheyeish lo matirin; but in davar she'ein lo matirin, in all rabbinic prohibitions one increases and nullifies — therefore he ruled b'dieved even if he intended to nullify them and did not know it is forbidden to do so, it is permitted — end of his words; and I proved above that if he did not know there is prohibition in the matter, it is permitted in all nullifications — end of his words.`,
    '6#ג': `Dry. Shach wrote: it appears to me to permit in great loss if it is dry in dry and min b'mino and it became known in between — see there, for he brings several proofs for his words; and all this if they have one taste; but two prohibitions that differ in taste — then each nullifies its fellow — see above siman 98. Taz wrote: one may say regarding inserting a spoon twice in siman 94 — here there is a new prohibition.`,
    '6#ד': `In meat. Shach wrote: one should not say it is plain, for every prohibition is nullified in sixty — one may explain because in siman 259 it will be explained that regarding heter b'heter nullification does not apply — it teaches us here we do not say thus; see there. Turei Chayim wrote that even l'chatchila it is permitted to put the water in a meat pot since it was already nullified.`,
    '7#_': `To use. Taz disagrees with this law and brings several proofs that we say this only where it also spoils; Bach and Levush ruled thus — see there; and see siman 122 seif 5.`
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `The bones of the prohibition, etc. For now l'fanei denu in Bavli and Yerushalmi like Ra'ah in Beit HaBechirah daf 96b and Ran chullin 98: soft bones combine with the prohibition because of marrow in them and because of their softness moisture emerges; but dry bones or eggshells and the like — even those of prohibition combine to nullify the prohibition; and so is the view of Issur VeHeter klal 28 in the name of Or Zarua, and so Bach.`,
    '1#ג': `For this is the essence. Bach wrote: it is astonishing — one should rule that bones of prohibition combine with prohibition, since they adhere to meat they first received prohibition taste and became nevelah; and he holds (above siman 98 seif 5) that even in something without taste of its own we say chein nafsho. There is no astonishment here, for presumably the Rav did not write above siman 98 seif 5 to be stringent in a new earthenware vessel that became nevelah even though it has no taste of its own — only because it has no remedy through hag'alah and the prohibition cannot be separated, as written there; if so a bone vessel that has remedy through hag'alah, as explained in Orach Chaim siman 451, certainly is like a metal vessel, for the Rav holds we do not say chein nafsho unless it is old, when what is absorbed in it became nevelah.`,
    '3#א': `The absorbed piece, etc. Explanation: the piece that absorbed from prohibition that fell into permitted food, and there is not sixty in the permitted food to nullify the prohibition except through combining the piece — they also combine the piece to nullify it; however the piece itself is forbidden, because the prohibition in it is not entirely emitted, as Rashba and Mechaber wrote at the beginning of siman 106; see siman 92 s.k. 10.`,
    '4#_': `Even though there was more permitted food at first and it diminished in cooking and was absorbed in the pot. Even so one does not estimate except as it comes before us — that in the prohibition too there was more and it diminished and was absorbed in the pot — end of Tur's words, and this is from Rashi and Rosh; and Maharshal wrote chullin 98 siman 34: it appears in my view the explanation is where we saw the prohibition fell, etc. — one should not estimate what the pot absorbed, for one cannot stand on the root of the matter and does not know how much the pot absorbed and how many times spoilage will come from this; rather one always estimates as it comes before us. Rashi did not rule thus as law, but nevertheless the essence is not to estimate except as it comes before us — such is the correct way l'chatchila — end of his words.`,
    '5#א': `One does not nullify prohibition l'chatchila. Rashba wrote in Taharat HaBayit bayit 4 beginning of sha'ar 3 and Ran chullin 98 that this is d'rabbanan according to Tosafot; but according to Raavad it is forbidden d'oraisa; however b'dieved even Raavad's view is as Mechaber wrote, and as Rashba and Ran wrote in his name — see there; and from the words of Rav R' Menachem Azariah in responsum siman 27 it is clear he holds like those who say it is d'rabbanan.`,
    '5#ג': `If b'shogeg it is permitted. And this is specifically in other prohibitions according to Tur, for we do not say chein nafsho in other prohibitions; but in basar b'chalav that became nevelah, even b'shogeg it is forbidden unless he increased sixty against all that became nevelah; and the Rav who holds chein nafsho in all prohibitions had to write specifically when dry mixed with dry, etc.`,
    '5#ד': `Forbidden for one who nullifies for himself, etc. And in Beit Yosef — it appears that if it was not his and he also did not intend to nullify for himself, it is permitted for him, since he did not permit for one for whom it was nullified for him and it was also not his — his bad act does not help — end of his words.`,
    '5#ח': `But a piece that absorbed prohibition, etc., for we say chein nafsho. At first glance this is difficult — what connection does this have to chein nafsho? For even according to those who do not say chein nafsho in other prohibitions, nevertheless a piece that absorbed prohibition and permitted food was added afterward — the piece itself remains in its prohibition, as Rashba and Mechaber wrote below siman 106, that the prohibition in it is not entirely emitted; rather the Rav comes to teach us that a piece that absorbed prohibition and permitted food was added afterward — even the addition is forbidden because there is not sixty against the entire piece; but if he increased b'shogeg sixty against all that became nevelah, even in basar b'chalav wet in wet all is permitted; but in a piece that absorbed prohibition, even if he increased sixty against the entire piece, the piece itself remains forbidden, as below siman 106 — see there. Nevertheless what is written here in Shulchan Aruch — Rashba's responsum siman 495 — is true, for so he wrote in dry mixture; see Rashba there that we do not say chein nafsho only in a piece absorbed from prohibition, but in wet in wet we establish that all prohibitions that one increased upon b'shogeg are permitted; and through this Tur's words are resolved, that he wrote one need not investigate if water was added after the prohibition was put in — for in basar b'chalav chein nafsho, and one would have to explain that in basar b'chalav one must investigate; only the reason is in wet in wet we do not say chein nafsho — end of his words. But one cannot say thus, for in Bavli chullin daf 108b regarding what they challenge there — why is milk permitted, it is nevelah milk, etc. — it is proven explicitly that even wet in wet chein nafsho applies in basar b'chalav; and so Rashba wrote in Taharat HaBayit daf 98 explicitly, and so it appears explicitly from Rosh's words end of chullin regarding hag'alah of a metal vessel and end of Avodah Zarah regarding yayin nesech that fell into a pit — we do not distinguish thus regarding chein nafsho — see there; and even though there are poskim who distinguish thus, as explained above siman 92 seif 4 — that is in other prohibitions, but in basar b'chalav one cannot distinguish thus; and furthermore Rashba and Tur presumably do not hold thus, as written; and what is written in the name of Rashba's responsum is not so, for otherwise he would contradict his own words in Taharat HaBayit and also be against Bavli; but one who examines Rashba's responsum there will see he wrote only regarding other prohibitions where we do not say chein nafsho, distinguishing between wet and piece — in wet, if afterward added up to sixty all is permitted, and in a piece it remains forbidden because the prohibition is not entirely emitted, as explained below siman 110; and this matter is not relevant here (and I settled Tur's words in my book another way — see there); if so the Rav's words require study.`,
    '5#ט': `And some say that even in a place, etc. Issur VeHeter klal 24 law 10 wrote explicitly that even where we say chein nafsho — such as wet in wet or wet in dry or dry in wet — there is also a distinction between known and not known; for even though it became nevelah once, since there was not sixty in permitted food against it because it was not known to him at that time and he does not taste it now — the taste of prohibition — we can say well that all permitted food combines to nullify it — end of his words; and Darkei Moshe and Turei Chayim klal 85 law 8 ruled like him; and accordingly what is written here in the hagahah — even in a place where we do not say chein nafsho, etc. — means: not only in a place where we say chein nafsho is there a distinction between known and not known, but even in a place where we do not say chein nafsho do not say that even if known it is permitted as long as he increased b'shogeg — rather whatever is known is forbidden; and so Issur VeHeter writes there, that in a place where we do not say chein nafsho there is also a distinction between known and not known; nevertheless Issur VeHeter's words and the Rav require study, for since we establish chein nafsho, immediately when it mixed it became nevelah — what difference if it was not known? Nevertheless immediately it is considered a nevelah piece, and we do not find any posek to distinguish between known and not known; and so it appears in Bavli chullin daf 108 that they challenge: why, when Rav said an olive's bulk of meat fell into a cauldron of milk — meat is forbidden and milk permitted — why is milk permitted, it is nevelah milk? Rashi explained: behold a small amount of milk absorbed in meat became nevelah since it was forbidden, and when it returned and was emitted in the rest of the milk it became min b'mino — prohibited milk in permitted milk, and Rav holds min b'mino is not nullified — end of his words; and they answer: in a boiling cauldron it absorbs absorption, emits does not emit — see there and investigate; and so it is proven from all poskim who challenged what we establish chein nafsho in all prohibitions from medumei and menasech, that they forbid only by calculation — and many such challenges, resolved with difficulty; and some ruled from this that in other prohibitions we do not say chein nafsho; and if so they should have resolved there that it deals without known — and so it is proven explicitly from what Semag and Tur wrote siman 94 seif 2: if one inserts a dairy spoon into a meat pot twice and it is not known in between — one needs sixty both times, because the first time the entire spoon became nevelah; and since it was not known in between it was not nullified and one needs again a second time to nullify, as written there; and so from Beit Yosef siman 92, Maharshal chullin siman 57, Bach, Derishah end of siman 98, and other Acharonim who wrote from what Rosh wrote in responsa on a pot that had twenty-nine olives of permitted food and an olive's bulk of blood fell in, and another pot that had thirty olives of permitted food and an olive's bulk of milk fell in and they became mixed b'shogeg — all is permitted because each nullifies the taste of the other; and it is proven he holds we do not say a piece became nevelah in other prohibitions; and if so Rosh came only to say each prohibition nullifies the taste of the other prohibition, but regarding chein nafsho he does not deal — perhaps he deals when they mixed together before the mixture was known, as Issur VeHeter himself explained there that responsum of Rosh — thus Or Zarua and not so; and so it appears explicitly from Beit Yosef in this siman regarding what Semak wrote that the sage must investigate, etc. — see there and investigate; also what Turei Chayim writes there proves Issur VeHeter's words, that even min b'mino that requires sixty d'rabbanan is not permitted except when increased b'shogeg — meaning it deals with wet in wet, for in dry one in two is nullified — end of his words; I did not reach the end of his view, for Tur follows his reasoning that he holds we do not say chein nafsho in other prohibitions; and so it appears from Maharai in Hagahat ShaDa and Maharshal chullin 99 who concluded and wrote that what we establish — all prohibitions that one increased upon b'shogeg are permitted — is specifically in a dry thing one should practice thus, but not in a moist thing that has noten taam, for we establish chein nafsho — end of his words; and we do not distinguish between known and not known (and what Issur VeHeter wrote that even wet in dry and added before the mixture was known — even the piece itself is permitted without hag'alah — is not so, as we establish below siman 106 that the prohibition is not entirely emitted from it — end of his words); and perhaps the Rav also retracted and therefore did not write this law in his hagahot — requires study. Also what Issur VeHeter writes there, that even in a place where we do not say chein nafsho, if known it is forbidden even b'shogeg, and he explains what Tur wrote: it is written in Sefer HaMitzvot that the sage must investigate if water was put in the pot after the prohibition was put in, that that water does not combine to nullify the prohibition — and it appears one need not, for we establish all prohibitions that one increased upon b'shogeg are permitted, and we need not be concerned lest he intended to increase b'mezid, for if we come to be concerned for this we also do not believe him when he says he did not increase — end of his words; that is without known in between, but if known Tur also concedes it is forbidden — and it is not clear; and furthermore if so why did he challenge Sefer HaMitzvot — perhaps Semak was concerned water was put in b'shogeg after it was known, as Issur VeHeter himself explained at the end of klal 37 Semak's words thus; and even though in Darkei Moshe he strained to resolve this, for Tur holds we are not concerned that if it were known in between he would immediately come to the sage to ask — nevertheless what Tur wrote "and we need not be concerned lest he intended to increase b'mezid," etc., is not said according to this — investigate (and in another version siman 11): Or Zarua — even if known in between it is permitted since he increased b'shogeg, and so it appears from poskim; and furthermore say: if you say even where chein nafsho is not relevant it is not permitted when increased b'shogeg except without known — if so the mishnah in chapter 5 of Terumot: a se'ah of terumah fell into less than a hundred of hullin and afterward more hullin fell in — if b'shogeg permitted, if b'mezid forbidden — and Yerushalmi says the same for all prohibitions increased b'shogeg permitted, b'mezid forbidden — and we say in many places all prohibitions increased b'shogeg are permitted, b'mezid forbidden — if so this b'shogeg must be explained before it was known, and if so how does it apply again to him? And if b'mezid — how is b'mezid relevant when he did not know? We must strain and say b'shogeg is before the mixture was known and b'mezid is after the mixture was known, for b'shogeg is like b'mezid — and this the mind cannot bear; Or Zarua as we said, and so Raavan explained end of Avodah Zarah daf 57b: Explanation — if they fell b'shogeg without intending to nullify, permitted; b'mezid intending to nullify, forbidden, for one does not nullify prohibition l'chatchila — end of his words; and so Semag negative commandments 87 (30a): Or Zarua — we do not distinguish at all between known and not known, but between b'shogeg and b'mezid, as it appears from Tur and all poskim.`,
    '5#י': `And accordingly, etc., and they did not practice thus. Meaning: accordingly, since the addition does not help — if so according to those who hold wet in wet chein nafsho, or a piece that absorbed prohibition for all, or the mixture was known according to Issur VeHeter — one would have had to investigate; only they did not practice to investigate in all this; and in Taz it appears he understood that this "and they did not practice thus" refers only to the first case — but this is certainly not so, for what difference is there? And so it is proven in Turei Chayim there law 7 as I explained; and the reason they did not practice to investigate appears to me that we do not establish prohibition presumptively — from where shall we say they added afterward? And in Turei Chayim there he wrote another reason — see there.`,
    '6#א': `Prohibition of their words, etc. In my books I expanded in explanation of the matters of prohibition of their words that has a root d'oraisa, per Rashba's and Tur's explanation; I explained everything correctly — see there; and one should not expand here, for it appears to me the essence for law is that even prohibitions of their words that have no root at all in Torah — one may not nullify or add to them, per the Rav's view, as I wrote there — see there.`,
    '6#ד': `It returns and is stirred. Peri Chadash extended at length on this root. See what Peleti wrote in all views of the poskim; for halachah one may not be lenient against Rama's words — see there.`,
    '6#ה': `Whether known in between or not. So Turei Chayim klal 85 law 10 in the name of Maharai in Hagahat ShaDa; but in truth it appears Maharai did not write there (Hagahat ShaDa 39) that even if known in between it is forbidden, but only regarding prohibition that gives taste, as he wrote there; and Raavyah forbids even if he knew — it appears from the reasoning of Ashiri that he told her it is a great reasoning, since the prohibition increased behold there is recognition and they sense the taste, etc. — end of his words; but regarding dry in dry min b'mino that was nullified in the majority when known — it does not combine to forbid, since d'oraisa and also d'rabbanan one in two is nullified — if so it was already nullified; and on the contrary it appears the prohibition also combines to raise the permitted food, as we taught (chapter 2 of Orlah) orlah raises orlah, and Yerushalmi explains that is when known; however in Ashiri chullin it appears explicitly that even dry in dry in its species returns and is forbidden even if known; but that mishnah of orlah is strained to resolve for Rosh, as I wrote in my book and wrote there the essence per Rosh's explanation chapter 2 of Orlah mishnah 3: even according to Rabbanan in other prohibitions in dry in dry, wherever there is no noten taam, knowledge causes heter and it no longer returns and is forbidden; and terumah is different because it requires separation; and so Raavad whom Ramban and Ran brought end of Avodah Zarah and Rashba in Taharat HaBayit — see there (and greater than this it appears Raavad's view there that even if not known in between it does not return and is forbidden, for the first is nullified even in something that gives taste); and even they do not disagree there except because taste is not nullified and behold the taste is sensed, and even in min b'mino where one cannot stand on taste — nevertheless since a measure fell there in the amount that gives taste in its counterpart in not its kind it is forbidden — end of his words; and above siman 134 s.k. 18, unlike dry in dry min b'mino; and furthermore Mordechai chapter HaGozel Eitzim in the name of Rashbam, Raavan, and Rivba, and so in Hagahat ShaDa siman 39 in the name of R' Yoel and Or Zarua, and so Hagahot Maimoniot chullin, and so in other poskim: a piece of prohibition that fell into sixty pieces of permitted food and was known and afterward another fell — the first is nullified first; and if so even though we do not establish thus except per Raavyah's view — that is because Maharai wrote it is a great reasoning since the taste is sensed, etc.; but in a dry thing min b'mino where this reasoning does not apply — if so whatever fell into the majority of permitted food and was nullified does not return and awaken, and on the contrary perhaps the prohibition also combines to raise; and so Issur VeHeter klal 24 law 10; but what he also ruled there that even if dry prohibition not in its species fell in sixty and was known and another prohibition fell — we say the first is nullified first, per Or Zarua and R' Yoel and their supporters — it appears we do not establish thus, for since taste in not its kind in dry requires sixty because if they cook them it will give taste — if so the same here; and it appears Issur VeHeter follows his reasoning that he holds in not its kind in dry the taste is not because if they cook them it will give taste, as he wrote in his name siman 98 s.k. 6; but per what he wrote there that the taste, as written — if so the same here; and that is what those poskim wrote that Raavyah forbids even in a piece of prohibition that fell into sixty pieces of permitted food, and it appears that is not in its kind in dry, for otherwise it should have said into sixty of permitted food; and Maharai wrote on this it is a great reasoning since the taste is sensed, etc., and that is as he explained that it gives taste when cooked; and that is what Hagahat ShaDa wrote, and Raavyah forbade even if he knew unless he removed them outside, etc.; and if wet in wet what help is removal, for in any case the prohibition emitted taste; and in Maharshal chullin 57 he wrote: but what Raavyah wrote unless he removed outside — Maharai does not hold with him, for since it gives taste in the pot, etc., what difference if he removed or not, etc. — end of his words; and it is not so except as I explained; and also from Maharshal's words there it appears per the Rav that even in dry in its species and known it returns and awakens and is forbidden — what shall I do, my view is void against theirs, and in particular I am lenient and they are stringent; however in great loss and the like one may be lenient from those reasons above, since it can be said what the Rav wrote "whether known in between or not" refers to where sixty is needed, as he wrote at the beginning such as when there was sixty, etc.; and so one may explain Maharshal's words there.`,
    '6#ו': `And afterward fell from the water, etc. And in Turei Chayim klal 85 law 10 Or Zarua wrote that even l'chatchila it is permitted to put water into a meat pot since it was already nullified; and one should not challenge on the substance of the law — it is obvious that every prohibition is nullified in sixty; one may say because below siman 259 s.k. 1 it is explained that heter in heter nullification does not apply — he teaches us here we do not say thus — see there; and so Issur VeHeter klal 24 law 8: we hear that heter in heter is nevertheless nullified; and if milk fell into water, etc.`
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `Forbidden bones combine. Since they are not edible they are not in the category of prohibition; nevertheless they absorb through cooking — therefore they combine to nullify the prohibition, for the prohibition spreads into them and they also have moisture to blend the prohibition absorbed from the forbidden meat. Maharach challenged Rama from what Turei Chayim wrote that I brought in siman 98 seif 5, that even pottery in a vessel needs sixty against it — if so, here too one should say the bones are nevelah, since they cling to the forbidden meat; and what differs from a piece of meat to which the heart clings and liver above, that that piece is nevelah? So too he challenged in Perishah. I say: Moses is true and his Torah is true — this bone is truly like a vessel, for we do not say chein nafsho in it if it is metal according to Mordechai, since it has a remedy through hag'alah; likewise this bone, for it is possible to separate what was absorbed in it now through cooking with meat upon it through hag'alah, for we establish in Orach Chaim siman 451 that bone vessels are permitted through hag'alah — therefore chein nafsho does not apply here, and it combines with the permitted food.`,
    '1#ב': `And the body of the pot, etc. Explanation: that same pot in which one is now cooking prohibition and permitted food; but plainly, a pot that was already forbidden and afterward one cooks permitted food in it — the body of the pot certainly combines with the prohibition; and on this Tur wrote above siman 98 to be stringent that the pot combines. The reason the body of the pot does not combine at all is that in the Gemara there are two versions regarding what combines to nullify: one says in the pot itself, in the pottery of the pot; and one says in what the pot absorbed and not in the pottery — automatically the latter version is stringent, as it appears from Rashi and as I wrote on this; and since they disputed the versions and it is a Torah prohibition we go to stringency and do not estimate the pot — and the essence of the matter we estimate as it comes before us and do not estimate what the pot absorbed, etc. — end of his words; it appears we establish not like both versions; but Rashba brings some commentators who hold that what he wrote "in the pot" is a stringency, meaning we estimate only what is inside the pot, i.e. the contents and gravy, but not what was absorbed in the pot; and the latter version is lenient, that even in what was absorbed we estimate; and we establish like the latter version, and that is like Rambam whom Shulchan Aruch brought in seif 4 that we estimate with a good estimate in what was absorbed; and this is also Rashba's view to decide thus in min b'mino; if so what Shulchan Aruch wrote "and the body of the pot does not combine" — that is the pottery alone and like the latter version; except that in the latter version we rely only in its kind; but in Tur one must say thus — or perhaps the pot does not combine at all, even what was absorbed; except that afterward he brings that Rashba disagrees and decides on this.`,
    '4#ב': `Even though there was more permitted food, etc. Rashba in his novellae and Ran wrote that if we saw the prohibition at the time it fell and it did not diminish by one measure together with the permitted food, we estimate the permitted food that was absorbed in the pot and combine it with what is inside the pot, and we estimate the prohibition as at the time it fell against the permitted food. And Rashal Pischei HaGolah siman 34 wrote to be stringent even when we saw, for one cannot stand on the root of the matter and does not know how much the pot absorbed — rather one always estimates as it comes before us — end of his words; and accordingly one may not rule permissibly in what Shulchan Aruch wrote in this seif "and we estimate with a good estimate," etc.`,
    '4#ג': `And this is only not its kind in its kind. For in this one needs sixty from the Torah, since it gives taste — therefore one cannot estimate what was absorbed; and some say: why do we permit now when there is sixty before us? Perhaps at the time the prohibition fell the prohibition was very large and much contracted from what the permitted food contracted — we do not establish prohibition presumptively since now there is no presumption and there is sixty; and through this is resolved what he extended in Derishah to challenge why we combine the bones with the permitted food though they do not diminish in cooking, and the prohibition may have been greater and diminished — for we presume as it is found so it was, in order not to establish presumption to say there was change in this pot initially from what there is now; and what Tur wrote that the prohibition too was greater, etc. — that is, since you wish to be lenient in this pot and consider also what was absorbed, since it is known there was more initially — if so we tell you that in the prohibition too there was more in this pot, since it is not like an ordinary pot — and in vain did the Rav toil in this.`,
    '5#א': `One does not nullify a prohibition l'chatchila, etc. In siman 84 seif 13 it is explained that if his intent is only for another matter, such as to fix honey, it is permitted; and in siman 137 seif 2 I wrote this is specifically when it cannot be done otherwise. And what he wrote here "he transgressed and it was nullified" or "he increased," etc. — it appears this is repetition, for nullification is increasing; and it appears his meaning is: he transgressed and nullified — he threw a small amount of prohibition into abundant permitted food where there is sixty against it, or it fell by itself when there was not sixty in the permitted food but he increased afterward. And what he wrote "if b'shogeg permitted" — one must say his meaning is: he transgressed b'mezid except that he thought this matter is permitted; and this supports what I wrote shortly that one who errs in law is called b'shogeg.`,
    '5#ב': `If b'shogeg it is permitted. Tur brought this in this language, that we establish all prohibitions that one increased upon b'shogeg are permitted; and Beit Yosef brought a source for this dispute of R' Meir and R' Yehuda and R' Yosi and R' Shimon regarding "they fell and were crushed," which Beit Yosef brings afterward on this page; and he challenged Tur's words against themselves, for here he ruled like R' Yosi and R' Shimon leniently that we do not penalize b'shogeg as b'mezid, and in siman 110 he rules specifically when it fell by itself, but if he cast it, even b'shogeg we penalize as b'mezid; and in my view it appears the source of this law is not from there but an explicit statement in Yerushalmi Terumot chapter Sa'ah Terumah — and these are his words: R' Avahu said in the name of R' Yochanan: all prohibitions that one increased upon b'shogeg are permitted and b'mezid forbidden — and accordingly there is no difficulty, for certainly R' Yochanan rules like R' Yosi and R' Shimon explicitly, and his reason is that wherever the permitted food depends on increase; and likewise in that case of "they fell and were crushed" — when they come before us we see crushed ones, and when he comes to ask it is permitted automatically since already increased or the significant matter was crushed; and if he was silent and said nothing we permit him, for perhaps at the time the prohibition fell there it was so and did not increase — only he revealed to us that it increased afterward; and therefore certainly he is believed that he was b'shogeg, for if he wished he could be silent — unlike that case in siman 110, when the mixture comes before us we forbid it, only he wishes to permit based on his testimony that he cast one to the sea — in this we do not believe him to be lenient on a clear prohibition based on his testimony; found his silence was not permitting; and proof for this distinction we find in Bekhorot daf 36 where we say there: what if you say there, if he wished he would not say, etc. — see there; and likewise in that case of "they fell and were crushed" we establish like R' Yosi leniently, for it is literally like this one who increases upon the prohibition; and this appears to me Taz's intent in Tur's words where he wrote "and it appears one need not that even if he added," etc., and we need not be concerned lest he intended to increase b'mezid (so copied the text in Rashal chapter 3 siman 59 and Turei Chayim general rule 5); and it appears to me Tur learned from Semag's words that certainly he is not concerned lest it increased after the prohibition fell there and stood a while — for if so the piece itself would have become nevelah like basar b'chalav and afterward adding water would not help unless there is sixty against the entire dish and also against the pot, and this is impossible; and in Beit Yosef it is written: if there was sixty in that water against all that is in the pot, Semag concedes it is permitted if he put them b'shogeg — and this is very astounding, for we also require sixty against the pot itself; and because of this Tur learned Semag's view that for this certainly we are not concerned, for we do not establish presumption anywhere to say it was in a way that would be forbidden, as I wrote above in this siman shortly; except that here there is concern in a matter where there is remedy to nullify the prohibition through increase — in this we suspect him that b'mezid he made the remedy; found here was the concern lest immediately when the prohibition fell he increased water there before the prohibition spread — like what we say in siman 92 regarding a drop of milk regarding stirring the pot; and therefore Semag said one must investigate afterward whether increase was made there; and on this Tur challenged: for what need to investigate? Granted if we penalized b'shogeg as b'mezid there would be reason to investigate if he says he increased b'shogeg — we tell him that even so it is forbidden, for we penalize b'shogeg as b'mezid; but in truth it is not so, rather b'shogeg is permitted for the reason we mentioned above; and on this he said we establish all prohibitions, etc.; and he said afterward "lest he intended to increase b'mezid" — meaning: that which we say all prohibitions that one increased b'shogeg are permitted — that is when we know it was done b'shogeg, such as done by another, or it is clear to us he did not know of the fall of the prohibition and increased — then permitted and we do not penalize b'shogeg as b'mezid; but here where it is not known to us — therefore even if he says it was done b'shogeg it is forbidden, lest he intended b'mezid and lies and says he is b'shogeg; and therefore we need to investigate whether any increase was made in order to forbid even if he says he was b'shogeg — this is not so, for if so you hold him a liar at the time of investigation — what does investigation help? Even if he says it did not increase he would not be believed if we suspect he lies. Therefore there is no need for investigation at all, even in basar b'chalav and a neighbor's, in other prohibitions; except that in other prohibitions we need not say it deals with increase immediately after the fall, for according to Tur's view we do not say the piece itself becomes nevelah — if so increase b'shogeg afterward also helps to remedy the prohibition; and in this Semag requires investigation of a sage lest he made the remedy of increase afterward; and on this Tur disagrees, for b'shogeg is permitted, as I mentioned regarding basar b'chalav if he increased immediately — found resolved the great wonder: how did Tur write b'shogeg permitted — behold in basar b'chalav we say the piece itself becomes nevelah, and Tur should have said but in basar b'chalav one needs investigation of a sage; and further resolved what he challenged in what Tur wrote "and we need not be concerned lest he intended to increase b'mezid," etc. — why do I need this, since he already brought we establish for permission from the Gemara — why challenge Semag here more than the Gemara; and according to what I wrote all works with heaven's help, and it is an explanation like a bolt and flower.`,
    '5#ד': `And if b'mezid, etc. It appears from Tur's language where he wrote here "if he did not intend to nullify the prohibition," etc., and likewise afterward where he wrote "but one who intends to increase," etc. — the essence depends on intent to increase: if he intended in this, even though he erred in law and thought it is permitted to increase to nullify the prohibition — nevertheless he is called b'mezid since he intended b'mezid to nullify the prohibition; and so it appears from Rashal's words which I will mention shortly s.k. 10; but in truth it does not appear so, for if so Tur is difficult where he wrote here that if we come to be concerned for this we also do not believe him when he says he did not increase, etc.; and if it were so that one who errs in law is called b'mezid since he increases with intent to nullify the prohibition — you would still challenge Tur on Semag: let us say one must investigate whether he did it b'mezid, that he intended to increase upon the prohibition to nullify it and erred in law and thought it is permitted — in this he would not lie and would say the truth, for there is no suspicion he committed an offense but a mistake — and why do we not investigate whether he erred or not? Rather it must be that this too is called b'shogeg; and proof from Shabbat chapter Kol HaGadol where we say in the first mishnah there: if he forgot there is prohibition of melachah in this, even though he knows today is Shabbat, he is called b'shogeg — likewise here; and one should not say there is some distinction between b'shogeg of Shabbat and b'shogeg of other prohibitions, for in chapter HaNizakin Gittin daf 53–54 they bring b'shogeg of Shabbat for other prohibitions in this. And further it appears to bring proof from responsa Mahari"k root 167 who ruled regarding a woman who committed adultery and did not know whether there is prohibition — if she is considered b'shogeg; and he toiled to find reasons she is not considered b'shogeg because in any case she intends to transgress with a man and commits adultery under him — for it is not written "if a man causes his wife to go astray" regarding intent specifically for prohibition, but "and commits adultery with her" is written — end of his words; learn from this in other places this reason does not apply and properly called b'shogeg if he erred in law; and therefore it appears to me what Tur wrote "but one who intends to increase" means he knows there is prohibition in the matter and nevertheless does it — this is called b'mezid; so it appears to me. Again I found thus in Tosafot Bekhorot daf 23 in the statement beginning "hold," etc., that even for one who holds we penalize b'shogeg as b'mezid in nullifying a prohibition — nevertheless b'shogeg who thinks it is permitted to nullify is permitted.`,
    '5#ה': `And they did not practice thus. And this is Tur's reason disagreeing with Semag who wrote one must ask if permitted food was added afterward; and Tur wrote one need not, etc.; and Rashal wrote Semag's reason: if added even b'shogeg it is forbidden where the piece itself became nevelah — if so what helps what was added? One needs afterward to nullify also the permitted food; and so Beit Yosef — except he corrected Tur's words who holds we do not say the piece itself becomes nevelah except in basar b'chalav — in this he did not correct, for nevertheless in basar b'chalav one would need to investigate if added afterward; but in my humble opinion Tur's words are correct from what I mentioned shortly, and we need not be concerned lest it was added after it became nevelah, for we do not establish prohibition presumptively, and as it is found so it was; and Semag was concerned only where the increase helps, as I mentioned, that in this a person is suspected to remedy b'mezid since in any case there is no prohibition; and Tur disagrees, as we mentioned; and so is the essence. However, if the teacher knows it was added but it is not known when — in such a case certainly one must investigate when it was added; so it appears for practical halachah, even though Rashal is stringent in every matter to ask afterward — nevertheless after the custom nothing except as I wrote in my humble opinion.`,
    '7#_': `If a small amount was absorbed, etc. Rashba in Taharat HaBayit daf 126 learned this law — and these are his words: from that which is stated in chapter Ein Ma'amidin: gentile vessels — gentile puts wine in them, Israel puts water in them, meaning and drinks them; and they asked there: what about putting beer in it? And we conclude it is permitted, for the text there is: R' Yitzchak son of Rav Yehuda permitted Rav Chiya to put beer in it and he went and put wine in it, and nevertheless he was not concerned to forbid beer because of wine — he said it is merely incidental; and the reason for this matter is that wine absorbed in a vessel is a small matter, for whatever is used in cold, its absorption is only small; and since so, and this vessel is used with permitted food abundantly, it is impossible to come to noten taam — therefore even l'chatchila permitted; and therefore a slight prohibition absorbed in the pot or in jugs and the like is permitted to use them l'chatchila even ben yomo, since it is impossible ever to come to noten taam; but if absorbed in a vessel whose way is to use it sometimes in a small amount, such as a bowl and the like — forbidden to use it even abundantly, a decree lest one use it in a small amount and come to noten taam — end of his words. And through this all work correctly. According to what is written above siman 92 seif 6. And this is what is written in a pot — for that in Pesachim or jugs as above, and not as Tosafot wrote in Pesachim there. And Tur's words are correct in my humble opinion. And the law is true law as I wrote.`
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 99 seif 1) A piece of nevelah with meat and bones that fell into a pot. The bones in it combine to nullify the prohibition — so too Rabbeinu Shimshon chapter 5 of Terumot, Rosh, Ran Pischei HaGolah, Rashba in Taharat HaBayit (daf 96 side 2), and Ra'avad in Hagahot chapter 13 of Laws of Terumot. From that which is stated in Yerushalmi: peels of prohibition raise the permitted food — and likewise for nevelah bones that have no taste — see there. And in my humble opinion it is clear they said only peels of prohibition that themselves are not prohibition; and since they have no taste they combine to nullify. But prohibition itself cannot combine to nullify even though it has no taste at all. Know that gid hanasheh — we establish there is no noten taam in sinews, and behold it is like wood; and nevertheless R' Nachman said in the Gemara Pischei HaGolah daf 97: gid in sixty and gid is not counted. And Rosh wrote: he refers to the mishnah who holds there is noten taam in sinews, or because of its fat — end of his words; and so the poskim, and Tur siman 100 seif 2, and so Rambam explicitly chapter 15 of Laws of Forbidden Foods: fat of the gid in sixty and the gid is not counted — only we require sixty of permitted food. And so in siman 90 regarding kchal: in chapter 1 it is counted because it itself is permitted, as Rashi and Ran explained at the beginning of the chapter; and in chapter 2, once forbidden, it no longer combines even though its entire substance is meat and it should be nullified in the milk — see there and investigate; and if so the law emerges for us regarding a limb from a live animal that has meat and bones that fell into a pot — the bones in it do not combine to nullify, since they themselves are prohibition and combine for lashes with an olive-volume, as stated in the Gemara end of chapter 3 daf 102 and Tosafot there daf 96 side 2 and Rambam chapter 5 of Laws of Forbidden Foods. And specifically in a piece of nevelah and the like whose bones are not forbidden, as Ran wrote, and one is not lashed for them unless with an olive-volume of meat, as Rambam wrote chapter 4 of Laws of Forbidden Foods halachah 18 — they combine to nullify; but not in a limb from a live animal, whose bones combine for an olive-volume of meat for lashes — they do not combine; and likewise in raising outside according to R' Yosi in Zevachim 107 side 2 and Menachot 23. And see Tosafot Zevachim daf 78. And see in Pri Megadim end of seif 2 whether sixty is needed to nullify sinews and bones in a limb from a live animal; and it appears for permission certainly they do not combine.`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 2) This is stated when it fell while it was raw; but if it was cooked first, the bones in it combine with the prohibition because they absorbed from the nevelah meat. It appears that for permitted food too it combines to nullify the prohibition, for Rashba wrote and Beit Yosef brings it in this siman and in siman 106, and so in Shulchan Aruch shortly seif 4 — according to R' Efraim we do not say chein nafsho in other prohibitions: a piece that absorbed prohibition combines to nullify the prohibition; and likewise for us, and even for us here regarding bones we do not say chein nafsho, as Taz and Shach wrote; and if so, even though they absorbed prohibition, nevertheless they combine to nullify, as Mechaber wrote in this siman; and likewise according to the hagahah in a place of loss — investigate. After I wrote I found thus in Lechem HaPanim in the name of Pri Chadash — praise to God.`,
    '3#_': `(There) But if it was cooked first, the bones in it combine with the prohibition because they absorbed from the nevelah meat when it was cooked alone. The Acharonim wrote likewise if the nevelah was already salted — the bones absorbed from the prohibition through the salting. And in my humble opinion specifically in bones that have marrow inside that are soft and absorb through and through; and as proven explicitly in Rashba whom Tur and the hagahah bring end of seif 1 — salting the bone helps for the marrow within it; and see in Mishnah Berurah general rule 15 s.k. 23. But in bones that have no marrow, behold they are like bone vessels, as Taz and Shach wrote; and it was already explained in siman 98 seif 4 in the hagahah that a vessel is not forbidden through salting except for the measure of a peel, and if cooked one need not estimate except against the peel; and see what I wrote there seif 4 similar to what Mechaber wrote here — see there and investigate. And in what I wrote it works well with what the poskim wondered: that which we say in the Gemara Pischei HaGolah daf 98 regarding the foreleg in Shiloh of a nazirite ram — according to one who holds the measure of prohibitions is in sixty, he holds meat and bones of the foreleg against meat and bones of the ram — we estimate and there is sixty and permitted; and these poskim wondered: why need to estimate against bones of the foreleg — behold in Yerushalmi the opposite is stated, that peels of prohibition raise the permitted food, as Shulchan Aruch wrote in this siman; and what Ran wrote that bones of the foreleg have marrow is insufficient according to Shulchan Aruch, for even bones that have marrow combine with permitted food — only the marrow in them combines with the prohibition; and Beer HaGolah's words here are not precise — see there. And according to what I wrote it is correct: in foreleg in Shiloh it must deal with that it was salted first because of blood, like the law of salting meat for a pot because of blood; and this is plain — if so, already the bones absorbed from the marrow within them and from the meat, for blood cannot be without grease — see Zevachim daf 26 and Rambam chapter 1 of Laws of Shechitah Chullin daf 8; and therefore one must estimate against them; and there is no proof at all from here to forbid if they were not salted in bones that have marrow — unlike Shach — see there; and all this appears clear and plain — investigate well.`,
    '4#_': `(Seif 4) We estimate the permitted food and the prohibition as they come before us, even though there was more permitted food initially and it diminished in cooking and was absorbed in the pot, since from the prohibition too was absorbed and diminished from the quantity it was. Rashi and Tur. It appears if the prohibition is meat of an old ox whose way is to diminish through cooking, and the permitted food is tender calf meat whose way is to expand through cooking, as stated in Uktzin chapter 2 mishnah 8 and brought in Menachot daf 54 — we do not estimate the prohibition as it came before us, for certainly it diminished from the quantity it was and the taste remained in the pot; and this is not comparable to what we taught in Taharot chapter 3 mishnah 4 and Rambam ruled chapter 4 of Laws of Fathers of Impurity: an olive-volume of nevelah that diminished — we follow the present regarding impurity — because the taste remained in the pot; and if the permitted food too is ox meat we say it diminished proportionally. And it appears to me: if the prohibition is calf meat that expanded through cooking and one must estimate against it as it is now; and if the gravy spilled and it is known there was sixty against it as it was when it fell, only it is not known whether there was sixty against it when it expanded — we go to leniency, for what expanded is rabbinic prohibition, as stated in Menachot daf 54; and see in responsa Radbaz vol. 3.`,
    '5#_': `(Seif 5) One does not nullify a prohibition l'chatchila. Noda B'Yehuda second edition part 14 siman 45 wrote: even those who permit nullifying a prohibition l'chatchila from the Torah — that is only such as foreleg in Shiloh where only taste is mixed, but to mix the substance of the prohibition such as dry in dry — for everyone forbidden from the Torah [and see in Ra'avad's explanation and Rosh chapter 1 of Orlah mishnah 6 it appears even mixtures dry in dry visible are forbidden only because of rabbinic decree]; and so in Mishnah Lamelech Laws of Me'ilah. But what he brings proof from Chullin 100 is no proof that forbidden in majority is stated below — for there it is majority not before us, as written in Yad HaMelech. Further one may say the sugya in Chullin according to what Ritva wrote in Chullin daf 98 on that which he said regarding foreleg in Shiloh: it is a novelty that one nullifies prohibition l'chatchila — for even for those who permit to nullify, etc., generally from the Torah — nevertheless it is a novelty in foreleg in Shiloh that its mitzvah is to nullify prohibition l'chatchila, unlike generally which is optional — until here; and through this the sugya daf 100 regarding sending the bird of the metzora is resolved: if it were forbidden, Torah would not command to send; and in Sefer Sha'arei Mitzvah Laws of Forbidden Foods he challenged Tosafot who permit nullifying prohibition from the Torah from Chullin 100 and did not mention the above distinction; and likewise one may say daf 115 regarding sending away the nest. And it appears also for everyone difficult, for we establish if he does not intend to nullify, for everyone permitted (and so in chapter 1 of Terumot, except Pri Chadash in Orach Chaim siman 473 forbids if the prohibition is recognizable and he benefits from it — but here he does not benefit from it itself; and perhaps Torah commanded to send so he should not intend to nullify). And according to what I wrote, all also I wrote what he challenged in Lekutei Shoshanim: why do I need the verse "every pure bird you may eat" to include the sent one that it is permitted — logically I know Torah did not command to send for harm; and see in Orach Chaim siman 626 that regarding a mitzvah we do not say ein mivtalin issur l'chatchila, since he intends for the mitzvah we do not decree lest he do the entire mitzvah in prohibition; and further because mitzvot were not given for benefit. And it appears difficult from foreleg in Shiloh: why is it heter that comes from a prohibition, that he nullifies the foreleg — behold it is a mitzvah to cook together; but according to what I wrote nevertheless why did Torah command specifically to nullify it; and further one may say there in Orach Chaim the reason is mitzvot were not given for benefit, as Agudah wrote — and this does not apply where there is bodily benefit.`,
    '6#_': `(There Taz s.k. 9) If he thinks it is permitted to increase upon prohibition he is called b'shogeg. One who errs in law is called b'shogeg and not called ones, even regarding oath and liable for a sacrifice, as Rashba wrote in his novellae chapter 7 of Shabbat. And it appears not specifically an ignoramus who did not know there is prohibition in this; for even a Torah scholar who erred in a matter worthy of doubt is called b'shogeg; only regarding examination he is called b'mezid in Even HaEzer siman 13, as written in Birkei Yosef in Choshen Mishpat siman 34. And so in Yerushalmi Gittin it is stated there: as one who thinks it is permitted to render terumah impure; and likewise a kohen who thinks it is permitted to make piggul is b'shogeg; and in Panim Me'irot vol. 2 siman 163 he brought proof from Bava Kama daf 95 and one who acts on a sage's ruling who erred is called b'shogeg even regarding examination, as Maggid Mishnah chapter 1 of Laws of Divorce wrote plainly: there is no greater b'shogeg than this; and unlike what Choshen Mishpat wrote in Even HaEzer that he is b'mezid; and likewise in Tosafot Eruvin 29 it is proven he is b'shogeg and we do not penalize b'shogeg as b'mezid for one who does not penalize; and so Magen Avraham in Orach Chaim siman 318, and so Tosafot in Sanhedrin 33, and behold proof, for one who acts on the ruling of a court brings a sacrifice, as Taz wrote in Even HaEzer end of seif 17; and so Beit Meir there in siman 13. And what the hagahah wrote there that he is ones — wrote in Birkei Yosef that this is only regarding ruling of the Great Court. And I saw in responsa R' Akiva Eiger siman 206 who rejected the words of a sage who permitted a woman bound to yibum who married because she erred in law and thought she was not bound; and that sage brought proof from Mahari"k general rule 68 regarding a designated maidservant who committed adultery through error in law — he forbade because nevertheless she transgresses with a man — it implies that without this she would be permitted; and Ra'avad answered him that we establish like Rava in Makkot who says permitted is close to b'mezid, and he did not mention that the sage's words are Taz's words here; and there are further proofs for this from Tosafot Bekhorot and Tosafot Eruvin.`,
    '7#_': `(Seif 6) A rabbinic prohibition one does not mix by hand. [See Ran chapter 3 of Pesachim regarding how we separate] But if it fell by itself and there is not enough permitted food to nullify it, one adds — so Tur in the name of Rashba; except he wrote rabbinic prohibition that has no Torah root; and Beit Yosef wondered on Tur: since even though Rashba initially wrote thus, behold he concluded one must be concerned for those who are stringent; and I wondered on Beit Yosef who wondered on him in vain, for one who examines well Rashba's words that Beit Yosef brings — it appears to the eyes that this did not enter Rashba's mind, not to add to a prohibition whose root is rabbinic — only he brought one view that even l'chatchila permitted to mix by hand; and on this he wrote one must be concerned for those who forbid mixing l'chatchila, but to add in this he did not say to be stringent, as Tur wrote; and again after I wrote the Rav Pri Chadash also challenged Beit Yosef on this, and so in Ma'aseh Rokeach, and so Rambam chapter 15 of Laws of Forbidden Foods wrote — and these are his words: milk that fell into a pot that has fowl meat and gives taste in it — one increases upon it other fowl meat and nullifies it, and he considers it has no Torah root; as Mordechai wrote and so R' Simchah whom Hagahot Maimoniot brings there and Beit Yosef brings.`
  },
  'peleti': {
    '1#א': `Bones of prohibition, etc. Ri Migash in the name of the Gaon brought that this is only species in its species, for Torah law is nullified in the majority, but in not its species not; and the master Pri Chadash upheld him — since they have no pelitah from their body they are like a pot, for we say "did permitted matter absorb and prohibition not?" — why should it combine to nullify, and why should the body of earthenware not combine? Therefore this is only species in its species, which is only a rabbinic measure; and the master in all five seifim wrote it does not appear thus according to Rashba and Rosh; and the correct view with them is: besides that they closed and did not distinguish — also they challenged from what we say in the Gemara according to one who says in sixty "meat with bones we estimate" — it appears bones do not combine to nullify prohibition. And according to Ri Migash from the difficulty — this is only an asmachta that the Sages learned for not its species, for in its species it is nullified in the majority and they did not say in sixty except because of taste of not its species; and in not its species in truth bones do not combine to nullify the prohibition.`,
    '1#ב': `And in a place of loss, etc. Bach challenged — behold this is clinging prohibition; and Shach explained a bone is like a metal vessel that hag'alah helps and chein nafsho does not apply; and according to what is written that there is pelitah — one may question: this pelitah — what of it, it is weak — nevertheless it became nevelah; but otherwise it is difficult — behold any taste to forbid clinging prohibition — perhaps in pouring it remains below in a way that there will not be sixty. And accordingly it is difficult — one may say if the sauce is forbidden and chein nafsho and the bone absorbs prohibition — if so one may say in all of it we estimate like a pot that absorbed prohibition; and well Bach challenged from this taste we forbid clinging; therefore in truth one must say it deals where the bone does not cling but rests in the pot.`,
    '4#_': `And this is species that is not its species, etc. This is Rashba's view and the reason of the matter — until here it deals where we know how much the prohibition was when it fell and we estimate against all of it; for otherwise even in its species one should not estimate — "did permitted matter absorb and prohibition not absorb?" — if so why not in not its species? One must say as if the pot had absorbed beforehand from water — why should that absorption not enter the calculation, for it absorbs and emits also; and one must say we are not certain what is absorbed in the pot whether it emitted or received part of prohibition — if so even here perhaps immediately before he put prohibition taste in permitted matter the pot already absorbed from permitted matter, and this does not enter the calculation as if it had absorbed beforehand — as written and afterward he puts prohibition in permitted matter taste and there is no sixty here; therefore permitted matter itself needs sixty — and this appears Rashba's reason to be stringent.`,
    '5#א': `Prohibition, etc. And Raavad's view is d'oraisa; and one should understand — since foreleg of a ram gives taste in a ram it is cooking of Torah prohibition, and it is a novelty that the Torah equated foreleg in stew — if so how did they permit cooking in a pot of a sin offering of peace offerings — as written foreleg with ram and as written sin offering and peace offerings — noten taam bar noten taam there is not, for there prohibition of sin offering regarding non-priests is like foreleg of ram regarding ram — why should it be called prohibition, and sin offering for peace offerings should be in the category of permitted matter — and as Tosafot and Rosh wrote kodshim vessels we establish on Torah law does not apply — behold forbidden to nullify Torah law; and one must say absorbed in a vessel is not considered nullifying prohibition until they said chein nafsho does not apply to it, and also complete prohibition name is not called upon it and it is not in the category of nullifying prohibition — unlike the name foreleg in ram which is well in the category of nullifying prohibition.`,
    '5#ב': `If b'shogeg it is permitted — Pri Chadash expanded to be stringent in not its species, for we establish in the Torah we penalize b'shogeg as b'mezid; according to one who says nullifying prohibition is only d'rabbanan — these matters are not understood at all that we penalize — if they permit b'shogeg he will do the act b'mezid and transgress Torah prohibition like cooking on Shabbat and planting in sheviit; but here what of it that it is Torah prohibition — nevertheless how do we decree if he nullifies b'shogeg he will nullify b'mezid — what of it, still there is no Torah prohibition, for from Torah it is permitted to nullify — and what of it that the prohibition itself is Torah; and true the Gemara's language is forced — for it says "behold thus when nuts split" from Torah one in two is nullified and acquired — R' Yosi — it appears where from Torah it is not nullified except in sixty well R' Yosi penalized; but in truth the Gemara challenges thus — even if we say forbidden to nullify Torah prohibition — if so one may say lest they come to nullify; and therefore the Gemara says: you may resolve if it is not yet nullified; but this that already was nullified from Torah stands one in two nullified — if so no longer applicable to nullify Torah law, for it is already nullified.`,
    '5#ג': `Permitted, etc. Taz in s.k. 8 challenged Tur's words against themselves — here he ruled Nachum penalized b'shogeg as b'mezid, and in siman 110 he ruled like R' Yosi who penalizes; and he explained to distinguish: all penalty lest he be b'mezid and say he was b'shogeg — if so where he has a migo of silence this does not apply; but in siman 110 it deals where the matter became known to the public — if so impossible to suppress the matter and he has no migo — therefore we penalize; and his words are unclear — for fell into the sea where they penalized b'shogeg as b'mezid stam teaches even between himself and it was not known at all to others — nevertheless they penalized even though he could hide the whole matter; in truth in siman 6 well explained that all Semag's concern requiring investigation is because perhaps he did not know there is prohibition inside and he added to the pot because afterward he is careful and also would tell the teacher of ruling thus it happened to me — if so b'shogeg in this for everyone they did not penalize as Rosh wrote below in siman 110 if he cast into the sea before the mixture was known it is permitted for everyone; and see Issur VeHeter klal 24.`,
    '5#ד': `And forbidden to sell it as well, etc. Shach's view regarding items permitted for benefit: if one does not sell to an Israelite at a higher price than he sold to a non-Jew it is permitted; and Pri Chadash's view is even in such a case permitted, for we establish regarding one who gave his animal to a non-Jew to castrate — permitted to sell to a non-Jew even though he sold it through castration at a high price, only he is compelled to sell it; and his words are unclear — for granted there if they had not castrated he could have kept it for himself, and now that they castrated he is compelled to sell — obvious that is a penalty; but here where prohibition mixed with permitted matter he was also compelled to sell to a non-Jew, for to an Israelite forbidden — and now that it was nullified if you permit him to sell to an Israelite at a high price — behold there is no penalty at all and he does not lose at all; and on the contrary he benefits and profits thereby — what penalty is in this? Therefore the correct view is Shach's words.`,
    '5#ה': `Or even moist in moist, etc. So too Derishah uPerishah to resolve Tur who wrote stam one need not have the teacher investigate when water was added — even if it is a ruling in basar b'chalav that for everyone chein nafsho applies — therefore in moist in moist we do not say chein nafsho; and Shach and Pri Chadash challenged in basar b'chalav certainly we say chein nafsho — and proof from milk became nevelah; and I already expanded on this above siman 92 in settling the matters. However with all this I did not understand Rama's explanation — a drop of milk that fell into a pot that has meat and sauce — if there was not sixty among all of it the meat is forbidden — if so if afterward they added water — true the sauce did not become chein nafsho for it is moist in moist — nevertheless the meat became chein nafsho; and if the teacher did not know and estimates as it is now, even the meat is permitted.`,
    '5#ו': `And some say, etc. Shach in s.k. 15 challenged Issur VeHeter in what he wrote even in a place of chein nafsho if not known in between and permitted matter increased — nevertheless permitted — for if so what does the Gemara challenge milk is nevelah milk — perhaps it deals where not known in between; and what he challenged from medumeh and vinegar — according to one who says chein nafsho in all prohibitions one may say not known in between — and so many more difficulties; and behold Issur VeHeter did not answer from his heart — only his words need to be said in Tosafot Bekhorot 22 s.v. yativ, etc., who wrote if there is no sixty of permitted matter relative to prohibition and yayin nesech fell in — if he adds permitted matter forever forbidden and other prohibitions permitted that he increases so much that there will be sixty against the permitted matter — and perhaps even in a piece we do not say first first nullified like a piece of meat that absorbed nevelah taste — even according to one who says chein nafsho in other prohibitions like basar b'chalav — we do not say piece became nevelah except pelitah does not help because impossible to squeeze; but increase helps — end; behold they wrote explicitly increase helps.`,
    '6#א': `Prohibition of their words, etc. One increases upon it and nullifies it; and the later ones challenged in Orach Chaim siman 676 regarding Chanukah lamp oil that mixed with other oil and there is no sixty to nullify it — one may not add to nullify it even though it is d'rabbanan; and strained to distinguish Chanukah is different for it was designated for its mitzvah — behold Tur there brought the law in the name of Maharam Rotenburg and wrote it is not similar to wood that fell from the palm — for there the prohibition matter is lightened; only he did not state the distinction that it was designated for its mitzvah — end; and I expanded on this in my novellae on the laws of Yom Tov.`,
    '6#ב': `Rabbinic prohibition. I already wrote resolution of what was difficult from Chanukah lamp; and I also wrote in novellae on Rambam on the laws of Yom Tov — we will seek and find at length. And in any case from Maharam Rotenburg's words that Tur brought who wrote Chanukah oil that mixed one may not add and it is not similar to wood that lightens acquisition — it is difficult behold Maharam holds chein nafsho in all prohibitions — if so the oil that mixed became all prohibition and if he comes to add he needs to add sixty against all of it — if so he is nullifying prohibition l'chatchila — and this even in wood is forbidden as Magen Avraham wrote in siman 504 see there; and here chein nafsho it is all prohibition — one must say he also holds in moist in moist it does not become nevelah — investigate.`,
    '6#ג': `It returns and is forbidden. Pri Chadash expanded at length on this root and stood on investigation to clarify; and I will bring his words briefly to clarify the law on clear grounds with Heaven's help. And first Rambam ruled in the laws of Terumah: a se'ah of terumah that fell into a hundred of hullin and he did not manage to remove it before another fell after knowledge — it is not forbidden; and if not it forbids. And from this Pri Chadash ruled Rambam's view in all prohibitions — after it was known we do not say returns and is reawakened neither before nor after; and he challenged Beit Yosef who wrote one may not learn from terumah — Rambam said only in terumah, but in other prohibitions it does not depend on knowledge at all.`
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `The piece, etc. Yerushalmi chapter 5 of Terumot: waving of terumah does not combine with terumah to forbid hullin, but waving of hullin combines with hullin to raise terumah; and in chapter 1 of Orlah and chapter 6 of Nazir — R' Avahu in the name of R' Yochanan: all prohibitions we estimate as if they were onion and garlic. What is the case? These say noten taam one in a hundred, and these say noten taam one in sixty. One who says in sixty — you do not make the foreleg one part in a hundred of the ram; one who says in a hundred — you make the foreleg one part in a hundred of the ram.`,
    '1#ב': `And the body of the pot, etc. Chapter 3 of Chullin 97b — R' Chiya: when they estimate, etc.; and we establish not like the first version, as Rashi wrote, and all the more so according to Rashba's explanation as written in seif 4 see there.`,
    '1#ג': `And some are stringent, etc. So too Rosh in chapter 3 of Chullin — from the baraita "there is no waving," etc. it appears neither for prohibition nor for heter; and furthermore for it says "but waving of hullin," etc. it appears but of terumah not; and so too it appears at first glance from the Yerushalmi there that says one who says in a hundred "you remove," etc. it appears it does not combine neither for prohibition nor for heter.`,
    '2#_': `In what case is this said, etc. — it is no worse than a pot, for we say in chapter 4 of Chullin 96b and chapter 3 of Chullin and chapter 25 — a pot in which one cooked, etc., b'noten taam.`,
    '4#_': `And they estimate the heter, etc. And this is, etc. — per Rashi there s.v. bamai, etc., and the essence of the matter, etc., meaning if we did not see the prohibition at the time it fell; and R' Chanina deals with where we saw the prohibition at the time it fell and it is now as it was and did not contract and the heter contracted. And Rashba wrote and it appears from his words that in such a case even what was consumed and lost through the fire also combines.`,
    '5#א': `And even if it fell, etc. Mishnah chapter 5 of Terumot, and afterward hullin fell there, etc.; and Yerushalmi there — R' Avahu in the name of R' Yochanan: all prohibitions that one increased upon b'shogeg are permitted, b'mezid forbidden; and it is not the mishnah — b'shogeg permitted, b'mezid forbidden — the mishnah comes regarding terumah; you may say even all other matters.`,
    '5#ב': `He transgressed, etc. Mishnah and the Yerushalmi there and Gemara in Gittin 54b, and like R' Yosi that the halachah is as him among his colleagues, as written in chapter 4 of Eruvin 46b.`,
    '5#ג': `For one who nullifies, etc. Like one who cooks on Shabbat according to R' Yosi — we establish like both of them because of penalty, as written in Gittin there 53; and they raise R' Yosi against R' Yosi: for it teaches one who cooks, etc. — for he did not penalize, etc.`,
    '5#ד': `And likewise for one for whom, etc. As written in chapter 3 of Yoreh De'ah 25a one who comes for the sake of this Israelite, etc.; and in Bava Metzia 90b he sent them trickery, etc. — obvious a grown son, etc.`,
    '5#ה': `And they are forbidden to sell it, etc. As the presumption is there they sell for slaughter; and even according to the conclusion there because otherwise he could benefit from it; and per Rashi there s.v. dayan, etc. — not so here; and as written in chapter 8 of Sheviit they sell to those who eat them and their value, etc.`,
    '5#ו': `And specifically, etc. As written above siman 92 — from this it is proven that in dry in dry we do not say chein nafsho; and so moist in moist in other prohibitions; and as written in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah 73b two cups, etc.`,
    '5#ז': `And some say that even, etc. He learned from what is written in chapter 5 of Terumot and he did not manage to remove, etc.; and they said in Yerushalmi there — R' Shimon said knowledge sanctifies it, and the Rabbis say raising sanctifies it.`,
    '6#א': `Prohibition of their words, etc. For they did not say in the first chapter of Yom Tov 4b wood that fell, etc., except that it fell into wood; and books add and increase, etc.`,
    '6#ב': `And some say there is not, etc. For what is written there "but in rabbinic matters one nullifies" — that is like the conclusion there where the prohibition matter is lightened.`,
    '6#ג': `It returns and is stirred. As written in Zevachim 31a "and piggul slept," etc.; and even one who permits there because of eruv thoughts; and in Bekhorot 22a R' Yosi said there is no language except, etc.`,
    '6#ד': `An olive of milk, etc. As written in chapter 5 of Terumot he removed it and it fell elsewhere, etc.; and the Sages say it does not cause meduma except, etc. — meaning and permitted entirely.`,
    '7#א': `If a small amount was absorbed, etc. As written in Avodah Zarah 33b they asked, etc.; and Tosafot s.v. shara, etc.; and Rashba wrote because the vessel's use is in cold and there is no absorption, etc., except a small amount, and they use the vessel abundantly and it is impossible to come to noten taam.`,
    '7#ב': `But if, etc. As written in Pesachim there — a decree lest he come, etc.; thus if its way is to use what forbids it is forbidden for everyone.`
  }
};
